## Why This Is Asked
Tests for autosave/drafts cover offline-first UX, idempotent writes, and cross-device conflicts, which are common pain points in real apps.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first data flows and write queuing
- Idempotent REST writes and ordering guarantees
- Test data isolation via seeds and reset hooks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright skeleton (pseudo)
test('autosave offline queue and merge', async ({ page, request }) => {
  await page.goto('/editor');
  await page.fill('#note', 'Initial');
  await page.waitForTimeout(31000); // wait for autosave
  // simulate offline
  await page.route('POST', '/api/drafts', route => route.abort());
  await page.fill('#note', 'Offline edit');
  await page.setOfflineMode(true);
  await page.fill('#note', 'More offline edits');
  await page.setOfflineMode(false);
  // verify upload order and no duplicates
  // reload or inspect draft store
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for WebSocket-based drafting?
- How would you test long offline periods and partial merges?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical E2E testing with per-user feature flags, data isolation, and telemetry verification in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Feature flags per-user, test data isolation, mocking services
- End-to-end UI verification and telemetry validation
- Flakiness prevention in a shared staging environment

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright example (high level)
const flagApi = new MockFlagService({ userA: true, userB: false });

test('enabled path renders tour', async ({ page }) => {
  await loginAs('userA');
  await flagApi.setUserFlag('userA', true);
  await page.goto('/onboarding');
  await expect(page.locator('.tour-step')).toBeVisible();
  // complete tour steps
